WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:03.930
Hello, I'm Megumi Seki from Kamoshika Fermentation.

2
00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:08.950
Today we begin the July session of Kyoto Prosumer College.

3
00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:12.370
July means shiso.

4
00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:17.270
If you made umeboshi, the umezu should be seeping out right about now.

5
00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:20.630
Here it is.

6
00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:24.850
Please taste a little of this liquid first.

7
00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:29.350
White umezu is surprisingly delicious.

8
00:00:29.370 --> 00:00:33.750
It's the essence of the ume, so the aroma is lovely too.

9
00:00:33.770 --> 00:00:35.710
Of course, it's salty.

10
00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:40.170
Especially freshly made white umezu.

11
00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:43.450
When anthocyanin meets acid,

12
00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:47.030
it turns an even brighter red.

13
00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:51.810
That's why you get such a beautiful red.

14
00:00:51.830 --> 00:00:56.290
Right when it goes in, it's just a pale pink.

15
00:00:56.910 --> 00:01:00.950
You can also cut it into thin strips.

16
00:01:00.970 --> 00:01:03.710
Pretty, isn't it? The ginger has turned bright red.

17
00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:09.290
I recommend making it along with your umeboshi, so you have it on hand.

18
00:01:09.910 --> 00:01:11.310
The color red

19
00:01:11.330 --> 00:01:12.670
always makes you feel happy.

20
00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:15.830
Red in the kitchen

21
00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:17.590
adds such a cheerful feeling, I think.

22
00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:23.090
And what brings red into the summer kitchen is red shiso.

